{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792712084,"nonce":"CLV1trhgxh1miGHncatz/ENMJxk=","message":"2026-10-23 Mac cold-start checkpoint.\n\nThe optimizer now computes dominators in dense reverse-postorder indices, avoiding per-iteration predecessor clones and map probes. The one-block case skips the dataflow setup. The dominator-only change produced identical optimized MIR for all 12 kernels; path-removal oracle tests cover every three-node graph, larger cyclic graphs, sparse IDs and nonzero entry IDs.\n\nFound a second shared cost: loop unswitching inserted LCSSA exit phis even when no split was applied. Trivial-phi elimination removed them, causing repeated full optimizer rounds. Both unswitching and unrolling now prepare a candidate and commit only successful transformations. Unrolling owns the exit-phi preparation it depends on. DeltaBlue goes from five outer optimizer rounds to three. Tests check unchanged ineligible loops and interpret the result of an unrolled loop whose induction value is used after exit.\n\nAdded benchmark_startup and CI artifacts for all 12 kernels, Cranelift and eager LLVM. Every sample has a fresh process and empty project cache, reports stdlib preparation/frontend/optimization/native initialization separately, and uses an observed stdout marker to measure process-start-to-main. Existing benchmark_runner compile_time_ms and warmup definitions are unchanged. RAYZOR_PROFILE_PASSES is diagnostic only.\n\nControlled comparison: before = HEAD 523a4da1 plus timing instrumentation; after = current optimizer. Same release features, four alternating samples per arm per kernel per backend, 192 fresh child processes. Both arms explicitly disable the embedded snapshot so the comparison isolates compiler work consistently; this is not a comparison of CI snapshot-backed startup. The sum of kernel median optimizer times falls 723.5 -\u003e 503.5 ms on Cranelift (30.4%) and 735.2